    "pros": [
      "Very low, wide step-through frame",
      "Gates belt drive and internal 5-speed hub: minimal maintenance",
      "Bosch Performance Line mid-drive",
      "Tektro hydraulic disc brakes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About $4,000 at US retailers",
      "130 kg total weight limit is lower than others",
      "Battery capacity not stated in the sources we found"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If budget allows, the Gazelle Arroyo C5 HMB Elite is the most refined, lowest-maintenance step-through here, with a belt drive and dealer support."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Bikes for Seniors",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-bike-for-seniors/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best electric bike for seniors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most older riders, we would start with the Aventon Pace 4 Step-Through, which pairs a low frame with a torque sensor and hydraulic brakes. On a budget, the Lectric XP 4.0 Step-Thru is about $999 and certified to UL 2849. For balance concerns, the Lectric XP Trike 2 or Rad RadTrike adds a third wheel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an electric trike better than an e-bike for seniors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A trike is better if balance at low speed or when stopped is your main concern, because it stays upright on its own. A two-wheel step-through e-bike is lighter, easier to store and more nimble, and it suits riders who still balance comfortably on a regular bicycle."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a step-through e-bike?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A step-through e-bike has a low, open frame so you can walk through it to mount instead of swinging your leg over the saddle. It is the most important feature for riders with limited hip or knee flexibility, and it makes quick dismounts easier too."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should seniors get a torque sensor or cadence sensor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A torque sensor usually suits older riders better, because it adds power in proportion to how hard you pedal, giving smooth, natural starts. Cadence sensors switch assist on once the pedals turn, which can feel abrupt. Some bikes, like the Aventon Pace 4, offer both modes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How fast do electric bikes for seniors go?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Class 1 and 2 e-bikes assist up to 20 mph, and Class 3 bikes up to 28 mph. Many older riders prefer lower settings; the Rad RadTrike is capped at 14 mph. Speed limits on paths vary, so check local rules and choose a class that fits where you ride."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does UL 2849 certification mean for an e-bike?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "UL 2849 is the US safety standard covering an e-bike's electrical system, battery and charger together. Certification means an accredited lab tested the design against fire and electrical hazards. The CPSC encourages buyers to choose certified e-bikes and to use only the supplied charger."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to talk to a doctor before riding an e-bike?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you have heart, balance, joint or vision concerns, or have not ridden in years, it is sensible to ask your doctor first. E-bikes are low-impact and many older riders benefit from them, but they still share space with traffic and require good balance and attention."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Outdoor Gear",
    tags: [
      "best electric bike for seniors",
      "step through ebike",
      "ebike for seniors",
      "electric trike for seniors",
      "best ebike for older riders"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated for 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Electric Bikes for Seniors</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Low step-through frames, upright seating and three-wheel stability, chosen on the details that make an e-bike easy to mount, comfortable to ride and safe to charge.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#aventon-pace-4-step-through"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Matters for Older Riders</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#bike-or-trike" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Two Wheels or Three</a>
          <a href="#sensors-classes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Torque vs Cadence, and E-Bike Classes</a>
          <a href="#fit-weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Fit, Weight and Storage</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Battery Safety and Riding Safely</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Running Cost and Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#who-should-wait" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Wait</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The right electric bike for an older rider is not the fastest or the one with the longest range. It is the one you can step through without lifting a leg high, start smoothly from a stop, put both feet down when you need to, and charge without worry. Those details decide whether an e-bike becomes a daily habit or sits in the garage.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run our own ride tests for this guide. We began with the step-through e-bikes and e-trikes that specialist reviewers and retailers recommend most often for older and less flexible riders, then read each manufacturer spec sheet and retailer listing for frame height, motor rating and torque, sensor type, battery capacity, bike weight, rider height range, payload and safety certification. Where a publication has weighed or range-tested a bike, we name the publication.
          </p>

          <p>
            We ranked ease of use above performance. A low step-over height, an upright riding position, smooth assist from a stop and reliable brakes matter more to most older riders than top speed or long range. We also gave weight to safety certification, because an e-bike battery is a large lithium-ion pack charged indoors, and to brand support, because spare parts and service keep a bike on the road for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            E-bike model names and prices change often. Brands refresh models every year or two, and older generations are frequently discounted or discontinued. Each card tells you where our specs came from and flags known conflicts, so you can match the listing in front of you to the bike we describe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing on this page is medical advice. Cycling is excellent low-impact exercise for many people, but if you have balance, heart, joint or vision concerns, talk to your doctor about whether riding is right for you and which type of bike suits your situation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Matters for Older Riders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the frame. A true step-through frame has a low, swooping top tube that you can walk over instead of swinging a leg across the saddle. That single feature removes the most common cause of awkward mounts and falls for riders with stiff hips or knees. Some frames are marketed as low-step but still sit high; look for a low, open center and, if possible, sit on one in a shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, think about riding position and comfort. An upright position with swept-back handlebars puts less weight on your wrists and shoulders and gives you a better view of traffic. Wide, cushioned saddles, suspension forks or seat posts and wider tires all soften the ride. Electra's Townie line, for example, is designed so riders can put both feet flat on the ground while seated, which many older riders find reassuring at stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at how the motor delivers power. Smooth, predictable assist matters more than raw wattage. A bike that surges forward when you push the pedal from a stop can catch you off balance. Torque sensors, which match assist to how hard you pedal, generally feel more natural than cadence sensors, which switch power on once the pedals turn. We explain the difference further down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider weight and storage. E-bikes are heavy; most of our picks weigh between about 50 and 90 pounds. If you need to lift the bike onto a car rack, up a step or into a shed, weight and the option to remove the battery matter. Trikes are especially heavy and wide, so measure your garage door and storage space before you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to look for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Frame</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">True low step-through</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mount without lifting your leg high</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Position</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Upright, swept-back bars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less strain on wrists, better view</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Assist</td>
